  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">LFN-DGD 22-26 SP and Labour Rights</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Farmers are said to perform the majority of the labor involved in producing food. The Lao PDR has a number of laws and policies in place to govern and advance farmworkers. All Lao residents, regardless of gender, are claimed to have the right to health insurance,the right to treatment, and the need to contribute to the National Health Insurance Fund, according to the law on social security.
It has been observed that there are still a lot of farmers and workers who are unable to obtain social welfare and who do not participate in the health insurance system. Because there are still numerous restrictions, such as the inability to access to information on different policies as quickly as they should and the lack of detailed research and analysis of written information. The primary workers in the production of food are thought to be farmers. Lao PDR has a number of laws and policies in place to support and managethose who work as farmers, including the Labor Law, Social Security Law, Health Insurance Law,and the Law on Gender Representation.
As a result, the Lao Farmers Network has a strategy to encourage and puts pressure on farmers to gain access to social protection policies, including social welfare work in informal labor groups that are farmers, especially to receive health insurance and access to health care, equally and universally. As well as generate knowledge about social protection with the intention and purpose of researching issues and studying solution, especially access to social welfare for the relevant parties, to educate farmers, especially women, to understand about social protection and to be able to promote social protection work. Access to high-quality social services is crucial for network members to be acknowledged and to promotefarmers especially women as leaders and problem-solvers in the community.
 Integrating the following primary activities: hold consultation sessions, gather farmer views on social protection concerns, particularly access to social welfare, and communicate the findings of the farmer point of view gathering to the relevant parties,Organize a policy consultation meeting on social protection  access to social welfare, formulate and disseminate policies on socialprotection work through social media: FB/Whatsapp/LFN Facebook LIVE, organise a study tour to share lessons with other experienced partners, Organise a meeting to introduce the project in the target province, choose community leaders and volunteers to speak on their behalf when sharing information with other groups, train them to showcase screenplays concerning access to social welfare, and prepare them to train community volunteers about social protection and accessibility. Community volunteers and community leaders are trained in communication, leadership, and collective bargaining skills by community leaders who also serve as village trainers.
Thisproject will work with members of the Farmers Network by identifying a total target group of 92 farmer organizations that are members of the network toparticipate in activities to research and study issues and propose issues related to social protection, especially access to social welfare for the parties concerned, and identify 3 provinces as the main targets for activities to strengthen andbuild skills for the farmer groups which have been set as follows: Champasak Province has set the Farmers&apos; Cooperative, Xiengkhouang Province has set the Organic Farming Cooperative in Paek District and Khun District has set the FASAP Association, in Vientiane Province has set the Chicken Bunch Group, the Cattle Breeding Group in Ban Geurn, and the Ban Jaeng Cooperative.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Lao Farmer Association</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Lao Farmers Network is a network of 50 farmers&#x2019; organisations from 13 provinces. The network has more than 2000 members. LFN was setup in 2014 by 17 farmers&#x2019; cooperatives with the purpose to strengthen cooperation among small-holder farmers.
LFN will contribute to all results in this programme, working with 9 community groups and 5 cooperatives by the end of the programme, totalling 1,570people, of which 60% women, located in 5 provinces. Building on their current experience of extending social security coverage to over 1.000 members, LFN will raise awareness and deliver new knowledge on social protection to farmers, especially in the most vulnerable rural areas. They will work with LFTU and other partners and stakeholders to strengthen the voice of Lao farmers for advocacy related to productivity, fair markets, income security as well as social protection for Lao farmers. LFN will also support networkingbetween small-scale farmer groups and grassroots trade unions.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
